@use-modules[(flora commands) (language scribble commands)]

@title{Foreword and Introduction 前言与导言}
@summary{Roger Angell的前言与E. B. White为1979年版所写的导言。}
@taxon["reference"]

@subtree[#:id "strunk1999-foreword"]{
  @title{Foreword 前言}

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{by Roger Angell}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{作者：Roger Angell}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{The first writer I watched at work was my stepfather, E. B. White. Each Tuesday morning, he would close his study door and sit down to write the "Notes and Comment" page for @emph{The New Yorker}. The task was familiar to him—he was required to file a few hundred words of editorial or personal commentary on some topic in or out of the news that week—but the sounds of his typewriter from his room came in hesitant bursts, with long silences in between. Hours went by. Summoned at last for lunch, he was silent and preoccupied, and soon excused himself to get back to the job. When the copy went off at last, in the afternoon RFD pouch—we were in Maine, a day's mail away from New York—he rarely seemed satisfied. "It isn't good enough," he said sometimes. "I wish it were better."}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{我观察到的第一位伏案写作的作家是我的继父E. B. White。每个星期二上午，他都会关上书房的门，坐下来为@emph{The New Yorker}写"Notes and Comment"专栏。这项任务对他并不陌生——他需要就那一周新闻内外某个话题，交上几百字的社论或个人评论——但从他房间里传来的打字机声却时断时续，其间还有漫长的沉寂。几个小时过去了。终于被叫去吃午饭时，他沉默寡言、心事重重，很快又告退回去继续工作。等到稿子终于在下午随RFD邮袋寄走时——我们当时在缅因州，距纽约有一天的邮程——他似乎很少感到满意。"It isn't good enough,"他有时会说。"I wish it were better."}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{Writing is hard, even for authors who do it all the time. Less frequent practitioners—the job applicant; the business executive with an annual report to get out; the high school senior with a Faulkner assignment; the graduate-school student with her thesis proposal; the writer of a letter of condolence—often get stuck in an awkward passage or find a muddle on their screens, and then blame themselves. What should be easy and flowing looks tangled or feeble or overblown—not what was meant at all. What's wrong with me, each one thinks. Why can't I get this right?}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{写作很难，即便是对那些一直在写的作家来说也是如此。不那么常动笔的人——求职者；要赶出一份年度报告的企业主管；要写福克纳作业的高三学生；要写论文开题的研究生；写吊唁信的人——常常会卡在某个别扭的段落里，或者发现自己在屏幕上写成一团乱麻，然后就开始自责。本该轻松流畅的东西却显得纠结、无力或浮夸——根本不是想要表达的意思。我到底怎么了，每个人都会这样想。我为什么就写不对呢？}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{It was this recurring question, put to himself, that must have inspired White to revive and add to a textbook by an English professor of his, Will Strunk Jr., that he had first read in college, and to get it published. The result, this quiet book, has been in print for forty years, and has offered more than ten million writers a helping hand. White knew that a compendium of specific tips—about singular and plural verbs, parentheses, the "that"-"which" scuffle, and many others—could clear up a recalcitrant sentence or subclause when quickly reconsulted, and that the larger principles needed to be kept in plain sight, like a wall sampler.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{正是这个反复向自己提出的问题，想必促使White去重新拾起并增补他大学时第一次读过的一位英语教授Will Strunk Jr.的教科书，并把它出版。结果，这本安静的小书已印行四十年，给一千多万名作家伸出了援手。White知道，一本具体建议的汇编——关于单数和复数动词、括号、"that"与"which"的纷争，以及许多其他问题——在需要快速查阅时，能理清一个倔强的句子或从句；他也知道，那些更大的原则需要像挂在墙上的箴言绣片一样，一直放在眼前。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{How simple they look, set down here in White's last chapter: "Write in a way that comes naturally." "Revise and rewrite." "Do not explain too much," and the rest; above all, the cleansing, clarion "Be clear." How often I have turned to them, in the book or in my mind, while trying to start or unblock or revise some piece of my own writing! They help—they really do. They work. They are the way.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{它们看上去多么简单，就写在White最后一章里："Write in a way that comes naturally.""Revise and rewrite.""Do not explain too much,"等等；而最重要的，是那句涤荡人心、清亮如号角的"Be clear."我多少次在自己想动笔、想冲破阻塞或修改某篇文字时，转向它们——无论是翻书还是凭记忆！它们有用——真的有用。它们奏效。它们就是正道。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{E. B. White's prose is celebrated for its ease and clarity—just think of @emph{Charlotte's Web}—but maintaining this standard required endless attention. When the new issue of @emph{The New Yorker} turned up in Maine, I sometimes saw him reading his "Comment" piece over to himself, with only a slightly different expression than the one he'd worn on the day it went off. Well, O.K., he seemed to be saying. At least I got the elements right.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{E. B. White的散文以从容和清晰著称——想想@emph{Charlotte's Web}就知道了——但要维持这一水准，需要无休止的用心。当新一期@emph{The New Yorker}寄到缅因州时，我有时会看到他独自重读自己的"Comment"文章，表情与他当天寄出稿子时只有细微不同。好吧，行，他似乎在说。至少我把那些要素弄对了。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{This edition has been modestly updated, with word processors and air conditioners making their first appearance among White's references, and with a light redistribution of genders to permit a feminine pronoun or female farmer to take their places among the males who once innocently served him. Sylvia Plath has knocked Keats out of the box, and I notice that "America" has become "this country" in a sample text, to forestall a subsequent and possibly demeaning "she" in the same paragraph. What is not here is anything about E-mail—the rules-free, lower-case flow that cheerfully keeps us in touch these days. E-mail is conversation, and it may be replacing the sweet and endless talking we once sustained (and tucked away) within the informal letter. But we are all writers and readers as well as communicators, with the need at times to please and satisfy ourselves (as White put it) with the clear and almost perfect thought.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{本版作了适度更新：文字处理器和空调首次出现在White的举例中；还轻微重新分配了性别，让一个阴性代词或一位女农场主得以跻身那些从前无辜地为他服务的男性之间。Sylvia Plath把Keats挤出了那个示例框；我也注意到，在一处示例文本里，"America"变成了"this country"，为的是避免同一段后面出现一个可能带有贬义的"she"。这里没有的，是关于E-mail的内容——那种不讲规则、全用小写、如今愉快地维系着我们联系的流动文字。E-mail是交谈，它或许正在取代我们曾经在非正式书信里延续（并珍藏）的那种甜蜜而无尽的谈话。但我们都是写作者和读者，同时也是交流者，有时需要（如White所说）用清晰而近乎完美的思想来取悦和满足自己。}
  }
}

@subtree[#:id "strunk1999-introduction"]{
  @title{Introduction 导言}

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{@emph{E. B. White wrote this introduction for the 1979 edition.}}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{@emph{E. B. White为1979年版写了这篇导言。}}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{At the close of the first World War, when I was a student at Cornell, I took a course called English 8. My professor was William Strunk Jr. A textbook required for the course was a slim volume called @emph{The Elements of Style}, whose author was the professor himself. The year was 1919. The book was known on the campus in those days as "the little book," with the stress on the word "little." It had been privately printed by the author.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{第一次世界大战结束时，我还是康奈尔大学的学生，选了一门叫English 8的课。我的教授是William Strunk Jr.。这门课要求的一本教材是一册薄薄的书，叫@emph{The Elements of Style}，作者就是教授本人。那是1919年。当年在校园里，这本书被称为"the little book"，重音落在"little"这个词上。它是作者私人印刷的。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{I passed the course, graduated from the university, and forgot the book but not the professor. Some thirty-eight years later, the book bobbed up again in my life when Macmillan commissioned me to revise it for the college market and the general trade. Meantime, Professor Strunk had died.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{我通过了这门课，从大学毕业，忘了那本书，却没有忘记那位教授。大约三十八年后，这本书又在我的生活中浮现：Macmillan委托我修订它，以供大学市场和一般读者市场使用。这期间，Strunk教授已经去世了。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{@emph{The Elements of Style}, when I reexamined it in 1957, seemed to me to contain rich deposits of gold. It was Will Strunk's @emph{parvum opus}, his attempt to cut the vast tangle of English rhetoric down to size and write its rules and principles on the head of a pin. Will himself had hung the tag "little" on the book; he referred to it sardonically and with secret pride as "the @emph{little} book," always giving the word "little" a special twist, as though he were putting a spin on a ball. In its original form, it was a forty-three page summation of the case for cleanliness, accuracy, and brevity in the use of English. Today, fifty-two years later, its vigor is unimpaired, and for sheer pith I think it probably sets a record that is not likely to be broken. Even after I got through tampering with it, it was still a tiny thing, a barely tarnished gem. Seven rules of usage, eleven principles of composition, a few matters of form, and a list of words and expressions commonly misused—that was the sum and substance of Professor Strunk's work. Somewhat audaciously, and in an attempt to give my publisher his money's worth, I added a chapter called "An Approach to Style," setting forth my own prejudices, my notions of error, my articles of faith. This chapter (Chapter V) is addressed particularly to those who feel that English prose composition is not only a necessary skill but a sensible pursuit as well—a way to spend one's days. I think Professor Strunk would not object to that.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{1957年我重新审视@emph{The Elements of Style}时，觉得它富含金矿。它是Will Strunk的@emph{parvum opus}，是他把英语修辞的巨大乱麻修剪到可控规模、把其规则和原则写在一根针尖上的尝试。Will自己给这本书挂上了"little"的标签；他会带着讥诮和暗藏的自豪称它为"the @emph{little} book"，总给"little"这个词一种特殊的拧转，仿佛在给一个球加旋转。在最初的形式中，它是一份四十三页的总结，为英语使用中的洁净、准确和简洁辩护。如今，五十二年后，它的活力丝毫未减；若论纯粹的凝练，我想它大概创下了一个不太可能被打破的纪录。即使在我对它动手改动之后，它仍然是一件小小的东西，一颗几乎没有失去光泽的宝石。七条用法规则、十一条写作原则、一些形式问题，以及一份常被误用的词与短语清单——这就是Strunk教授著作的全部内容。我有些大胆地，为了对得起出版商，加了一章叫"An Approach to Style"，陈述我自己的偏见、我对错误的看法、我的信条。这一章（第五章）特别针对那些认为英语散文写作不仅是一项必要技能、也是一种明智追求的人——一种度过时日的方式。我想Strunk教授不会反对这一点。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{A second edition of the book was published in 1972. I have now completed a third revision. Chapter IV has been refurbished with words and expressions of a recent vintage; four rules of usage have been added to Chapter I. Fresh examples have been added to some of the rules and principles, amplification has reared its head in a few places in the text where I felt an assault could successfully be made on the bastions of its brevity, and in general the book has received a thorough overhaul—to correct errors, delete bewhiskered entries, and enliven the argument.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{这本书的第二版于1972年出版。我现在完成了第三次修订。第四章换上了新近流行的词与短语；第一章增加了四条用法规则。一些规则和原则增添了新的例句；在几处我觉得可以成功攻破其简洁壁垒的地方，文本中冒出了扩充的内容；总的来说，这本书经过了彻底的检修——改正错误、删去陈旧的条目、让论述更有生气。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{Professor Strunk was a positive man. His book contains rules of grammar phrased as direct orders. In the main I have not tried to soften his commands, or modify his pronouncements, or remove the special objects of his scorn. I have tried, instead, to preserve the flavor of his discontent while slightly enlarging the scope of the discussion. @emph{The Elements of Style} does not pretend to survey the whole field. Rather it proposes to give in brief space the principal requirements of plain English style. It concentrates on fundamentals: the rules of usage and principles of composition most commonly violated.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{Strunk教授是个态度果断的人。他书中的语法规则都以直接命令的口吻措辞。大体上，我无意软化他的命令、修改他的论断，或删去他特别鄙视的对象。相反，我力图在稍稍扩大讨论范围的同时，保留他那份不满的滋味。@emph{The Elements of Style}并不假装要纵览整个领域。它只是想在有限的篇幅里，给出平实英语文体的主要要求。它专注于根本：最常被违反的用法规则和写作原则。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{The reader will soon discover that these rules and principles are in the form of sharp commands, Sergeant Strunk snapping orders to his platoon. "Do not join independent clauses with a comma." (@link["strunk1999-rule-5"]{Rule 5}.) "Do not break sentences in two." (@link["strunk1999-rule-6"]{Rule 6}.) "Use the active voice." (@link["strunk1999-rule-14"]{Rule 14}.) "Omit needless words." (@link["strunk1999-rule-17"]{Rule 17}.) "Avoid a succession of loose sentences." (@link["strunk1999-rule-18"]{Rule 18}.) "In summaries, keep to one tense." (@link["strunk1999-rule-21"]{Rule 21}.) Each rule or principle is followed by a short hortatory essay, and usually the exhortation is followed by, or interlarded with, examples in parallel columns—the true vs. the false, the right vs. the wrong, the timid vs. the bold, the ragged vs. the trim. From every line there peers out at me the puckish face of my professor, his short hair parted neatly in the middle and combed down over his forehead, his eyes blinking incessantly behind steel-rimmed spectacles as though he had just emerged into strong light, his lips nibbling each other like nervous horses, his smile shuttling to and fro under a carefully edged mustache. "Omit needless words!" cries the author on page 23, and into that imperative Will Strunk really put his heart and soul. In the days when I was sitting in his class, he omitted so many needless words, and omitted them so forcibly and with such eagerness and obvious relish, that he often seemed in the position of having shortchanged himself—a man left with nothing more to say yet with time to fill, a radio prophet who had outdistanced the clock. Will Strunk got out of this predicament by a simple trick: he uttered every sentence three times. When he delivered his oration on brevity to the class, he leaned forward over his desk, grasped his coat lapels in his hands, and, in a husky, conspiratorial voice, said, "Rule Seventeen. Omit needless words! Omit needless words! Omit needless words!" He was a memorable man, friendly and funny. Under the remembered sting of his kindly lash, I have been trying to omit needless words since 1919, and although there are still many words that cry for omission and the huge task will never be accomplished, it is exciting to me to reread the masterly Strunkian elaboration of this noble theme. It goes:}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{读者很快就会发现，这些规则和原则都以尖锐命令的形式出现，是Strunk中士在向他的排发号施令。"Do not join independent clauses with a comma."（@link["strunk1999-rule-5"]{规则5}。）"Do not break sentences in two."（@link["strunk1999-rule-6"]{规则6}。）"Use the active voice."（@link["strunk1999-rule-14"]{规则14}。）"Omit needless words."（@link["strunk1999-rule-17"]{规则17}。）"Avoid a succession of loose sentences."（@link["strunk1999-rule-18"]{规则18}。）"In summaries, keep to one tense."（@link["strunk1999-rule-21"]{规则21}。）每条规则或原则后面都跟着一篇简短的劝诫短文，而这劝诫之后，或其间，通常还夹杂着并排对照的例句——真对假、对与错、怯懦对大胆、杂乱对整洁。我教授那张顽皮的脸从每一行文字里向外望着我：他的短发从正中整齐分开，梳下来盖住前额；眼睛在钢丝边眼镜后面不停地眨，仿佛他刚走进强光里；他的双唇像紧张的马一样互相啃咬；他的微笑在精心修出棱角的胡髭下穿梭不已。"Omit needless words!"作者在第23页喊道，而在那句祈使命令里，Will Strunk真地倾注了心血与灵魂。我坐在他课堂里的那些日子，他省去了那么多不必要的词，而且省得那么有力、那么急切、那么明显津津有味，以至于他常常看起来像是在亏待自己——一个再没什么可说、却还有时间要填满的人，一个跑在了时钟前头的电台先知。Will Strunk用一个简单的把戏摆脱了这种窘境：他把每句话都说三遍。当他向全班发表关于简洁的演说时，他俯身趴在讲桌上，双手抓住自己外套的翻领，用沙哑而神秘的声音说："Rule Seventeen. Omit needless words! Omit needless words! Omit needless words!"他是个令人难忘的人，友善而风趣。在他那仁慈鞭子的令人记忆犹新的刺痛之下，我从1919年起就一直努力省去不必要的词；尽管仍有许多词在那里呼唤着要被删掉，这项巨大的任务也永远不会完成，但重读Strunk对这一崇高主题的大师级阐发，仍令我兴奋。它是这样写的：}
  }

  @blockquote{Vigorous writing is concise. A sentence should contain no unnecessary words, a paragraph no unnecessary sentences, for the same reason that a drawing should have no unnecessary lines and a machine no unnecessary parts. This requires not that the writer make all sentences short or avoid all detail and treat subjects only in outline, but that every word tell.}

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{There you have a short, valuable essay on the nature and beauty of brevity—fifty-nine words that could change the world. Having recovered from his adventure in prolixity (fifty-nine words were a lot of words in the tight world of William Strunk Jr.), the professor proceeds to give a few quick lessons in pruning. Students learn to cut the dead wood from "this is a subject that," reducing it to "this subject," a saving of three words. They learn to trim "used for fuel purposes" down to "used for fuel." They learn that they are being chatterboxes when they say "the question as to whether" and that they should just say "whether"—a saving of four words out of a possible five.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{你在这里看到了一篇简短而有价值的小文，论简洁的本质与美——五十九个词，足以改变世界。从他在冗长中的这次冒险（在William Strunk Jr.狭小的世界里，五十九个词已是很多词）恢复过来之后，教授接着给出了几堂快速的删削课。学生们学会删去"this is a subject that"里的死木头，把它缩减为"this subject"，省下三个词。他们学会把"used for fuel purposes"修剪成"used for fuel"。他们学会，说"the question as to whether"时自己就是在唠叨，应该只说"whether"——在可能的五个词里省下四个。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{The professor devotes a special paragraph to the vile expression @emph{the fact that}, a phrase that causes him to quiver with revulsion. The expression, he says, should be "revised out of every sentence in which it occurs." But a shadow of gloom seems to hang over the page, and you feel that he knows how hopeless his cause is. I suppose I have written @emph{the fact that} a thousand times in the heat of composition, revised it out maybe five hundred times in the cool aftermath. To be batting only .500 this late in the season, to fail half the time to connect with this fat pitch, saddens me, for it seems a betrayal of the man who showed me how to swing at it and made the swinging seem worthwhile.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{教授专门用一段来谈@emph{the fact that}这个可憎的表达，这个短语让他厌恶得发抖。他说，这个表达应该"revised out of every sentence in which it occurs."。但那一页上似乎笼罩着一层阴郁，你能感觉到他知道自己的主张多么无望。我想，我在写作的热头上写过一千次@emph{the fact that}，在冷静下来之后大概删掉了五百次。赛季这么晚还只打到.500的打击率，一半的时间都打不中这个好球，让我难过，因为这似乎是对那个教我怎么挥棒、并让挥棒显得值得的人的背叛。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{I treasure @emph{The Elements of Style} for its sharp advice, but I treasure it even more for the audacity and self-confidence of its author. Will knew where he stood. He was so sure of where he stood, and made his position so clear and so plausible, that his peculiar stance has continued to invigorate me—and, I am sure, thousands of other ex-students—during the years that have intervened since our first encounter. He had a number of likes and dislikes that were almost as whimsical as the choice of a necktie, yet he made them seem utterly convincing. He disliked the word @emph{forceful} and advised us to use @emph{forcible} instead. He felt that the word @emph{clever} was greatly overused: "It is best restricted to ingenuity displayed in small matters." He despised the expression @emph{student body}, which he termed gruesome, and made a special trip downtown to the @emph{Alumni News} office one day to protest the expression and suggest that @emph{studentry} be substituted—a coinage of his own, which he felt was similar to @emph{citizenry}. I am told that the @emph{News} editor was so charmed by the visit, if not by the word, that he ordered the student body buried, never to rise again. @emph{Studentry} has taken its place. It's not much of an improvement, but it does sound less cadaverous, and it made Will Strunk quite happy.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{我珍视@emph{The Elements of Style}，因为它的建议犀利；但我更珍视它，因为其作者的大胆和自信。Will知道自己站在哪里。他对自己的立场如此确定，把自己的立场表达得如此清晰、如此令人信服，以至于他那独特的姿态一直激励着我——我确信，也激励着成千上万其他旧日学生——在我们初次相遇之后流逝的那些岁月里。他有许多喜欢和不喜欢，近乎像挑选领带一样随心所欲，可他让它们显得完全令人信服。他不喜欢@emph{forceful}这个词，建议我们改用@emph{forcible}。他觉得@emph{clever}这个词被用得太滥："It is best restricted to ingenuity displayed in small matters."。他鄙视@emph{student body}这个说法，称之为令人毛骨悚然，还专门进城到@emph{Alumni News}办公室去抗议这个说法，并建议改用@emph{studentry}——那是他自己造的词，他觉得它类似于@emph{citizenry}。我听说@emph{News}的编辑被这次来访迷住了（如果不是被那个词迷住的话），于是下令把student body埋葬，永世不得翻身。@emph{Studentry}取代了它的位置。这算不上多大的改进，但它听起来确实没那么像尸体，这让Will Strunk相当高兴。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{Some years ago, when the heir to the throne of England was a child, I noticed a headline in the @emph{Times} about Bonnie Prince Charlie: "Charles' tonsils out." Immediately Rule 1 leapt to mind.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{几年前，当英国王位继承人还是个孩子时，我注意到@emph{Times}上一则关于Bonnie Prince Charlie的标题："Charles' tonsils out."。规则1立刻跃入脑海。}
  }

  @blockquote{1. Form the possessive singular of nouns by adding 's. Follow this rule whatever the final consonant. Thus write, Charles's friend Burns's poems the witch's malice}

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{Clearly, Will Strunk had foreseen, as far back as 1918, the dangerous tonsillectomy of a prince, in which the surgeon removes the tonsils and the @emph{Times} copy desk removes the final @emph{s}. He started his book with it. I commend @link["strunk1999-rule-1"]{Rule 1} to the @emph{Times}, and I trust that Charles's throat, not Charles' throat, is in fine shape today.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{显然，Will Strunk早在1918年就预见到了那位王子危险的扁桃体切除手术：外科医生摘除扁桃体，而@emph{Times}的编辑台则摘除了结尾的@emph{s}。他用这条规则开了头。我把@link["strunk1999-rule-1"]{规则1}推荐给@emph{Times}；我也相信，今天状态良好的应该是Charles's throat，而不是Charles' throat。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{Style rules of this sort are, of course, somewhat a matter of individual preference, and even the established rules of grammar are open to challenge. Professor Strunk, although one of the most inflexible and choosy of men, was quick to acknowledge the fallacy of inflexibility and the danger of doctrine. "It is an old observation," he wrote, "that the best writers sometimes disregard the rules of rhetoric. When they do so, however, the reader will usually find in the sentence some compensating merit, attained at the cost of the violation. Unless he is certain of doing as well, he will probably do best to follow the rules."}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{这类文体规则当然多少属于个人偏好问题，就连既定的语法规则也并非不可质疑。Strunk教授虽然是最不灵活、最挑剔的人之一，却很快承认了不灵活的谬误和教条的危险。"It is an old observation,"他写道，"that the best writers sometimes disregard the rules of rhetoric. When they do so, however, the reader will usually find in the sentence some compensating merit, attained at the cost of the violation. Unless he is certain of doing as well, he will probably do best to follow the rules."}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{It is encouraging to see how perfectly a book, even a dusty rule book, perpetuates and extends the spirit of a man. Will Strunk loved the clear, the brief, the bold, and his book is clear, brief, bold. Boldness is perhaps its chief distinguishing mark. On page 26, explaining one of his parallels, he says, "The lefthand version gives the impression that the writer is undecided or timid, apparently unable or afraid to choose one form of expression and hold to it." And his original Rule 11 was "Make definite assertions." That was Will all over. He scorned the vague, the tame, the colorless, the irresolute. He felt it was worse to be irresolute than to be wrong. I remember a day in class when he leaned far forward, in his characteristic pose—the pose of a man about to impart a secret—and croaked, "If you don't know how to pronounce a word, say it loud! If you don't know how to pronounce a word, say it loud!" This comical piece of advice struck me as sound at the time, and I still respect it. Why compound ignorance with inaudibility? Why run and hide?}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{看到一本书——哪怕是一本落满灰尘的规则书——竟能如此完美地延续并扩展一个人的精神，令人鼓舞。Will Strunk爱清晰、爱简短、爱大胆，而他的书就清晰、简短、大胆。大胆也许是它最主要的标志。在第26页，他解释自己的一组对照例句时说："The lefthand version gives the impression that the writer is undecided or timid, apparently unable or afraid to choose one form of expression and hold to it."而他最初的规则11是"Make definite assertions."那正是Will的作风。他蔑视含糊、温顺、苍白、犹豫。他觉得犹豫比犯错更糟。我记得有一天在课堂上，他极力前倾，摆出他那种标志性的姿势——一个正要吐露秘密的人的姿势——然后用沙哑的声音说："If you don't know how to pronounce a word, say it loud! If you don't know how to pronounce a word, say it loud!"这条滑稽的建议在当时我就觉得有道理，至今我仍然尊重它。何必把无知和听不见叠加在一起呢？何必逃跑躲藏呢？}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{All through @emph{The Elements of Style} one finds evidences of the author's deep sympathy for the reader. Will felt that the reader was in serious trouble most of the time, floundering in a swamp, and that it was the duty of anyone attempting to write English to drain this swamp quickly and get the reader up on dry ground, or at least to throw a rope. In revising the text, I have tried to hold steadily in mind this belief of his, this concern for the bewildered reader.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{通读@emph{The Elements of Style}，处处可见作者对读者的深切同情。Will觉得读者大多数时候都身处严重的麻烦之中，在沼泽里挣扎；而任何试图用英语写作的人，都有责任迅速排干这片沼泽，把读者扶上干地，或者至少抛过去一根绳子。在修订文本时，我一直努力把他这个信念、这份对困惑读者的关切，稳稳放在心里。}
  }

  @bi{
    @lang["en"]{In the English classes of today, "the little book" is surrounded by longer, lower textbooks—books with permissive steering and automatic transitions. Perhaps the book has become something of a curiosity. To me, it still seems to maintain its original poise, standing, in a drafty time, erect, resolute, and assured. I still find the Strunkian wisdom a comfort, the Strunkian humor a delight, and the Strunkian attitude toward right-and-wrong a blessing undisguised.}
    @lang["zh-CN"]{在今天的英语课堂上，"the little book"被更长、更浅的教科书包围着——那些书带着宽容的转向和自动的过渡。也许这本书已经成了一件有点稀奇的东西。对我来说，它似乎依然保持着最初的从容，在一个漏风的时代里，挺立、坚决、自信。我仍然觉得Strunk式的智慧是一种慰藉，Strunk式的幽默是一种愉悦，而Strunk式对是非的态度是一种毫不掩饰的福气。}
  }

  E. B. White
}
